P["subtitles-vs-captions-premiere-pro"] = dict(
modified='2026-11-08',
title="Subtitles vs Captions in Premiere Pro: What's the Difference?",
desc="Premiere Pro treats subtitles and closed captions differently. Which format to pick, what each one can do, and how to switch between them.",
h1="Subtitles vs captions in Premiere Pro",
rel=["how-to-add-captions-in-premiere-pro","closed-captions-premiere-pro","how-to-add-subtitles-in-premiere-pro"],
body="""
<p>In Premiere Pro, <strong>Subtitle</strong> is a flexible caption-track format that you can burn into the picture or export as a separate subtitle file. CEA-608 and CEA-708 are closed-caption formats used in specified delivery workflows. Choose the format and export method your client or platform needs; an SRT file can remain selectable in a compatible player.</p>
<p class="cta-line">Native caption tracks do not automatically move a highlight through a multi-word line. <a href="https://captionsgen.com/">Captions Gen</a> generates timed, animated graphics for that look. It transcribes locally on your CPU and places editable Motion Graphics clips on the Premiere timeline. The current Windows release is free, without a transcription time cap or watermark; Adobe Premiere Pro is separate.</p>

<h2>What Premiere means by subtitles</h2>
<p>When you click Create captions and pick Subtitle as the format, you get a caption track that you can style freely: any font, size, color, outline and background box. On export you can burn it into the video or save it as a sidecar .srt. Use burn-in when you want the exported video to retain the chosen appearance.</p>

<h2>What Premiere means by closed captions</h2>
<p>CEA-608 and CEA-708 are broadcast standards. The text is stored as data alongside the video, and the player decides whether to display it. Styling is limited by the standard, which is why your fancy font disappears when you pick this format. Broadcasters, streaming services and some accessibility requirements ask for it by name.</p>

<h2>Which one should you use?</h2>
<p>Ask where the video is going. Use Subtitle with burn-in for a fixed visual treatment, or a supported sidecar such as SRT for selectable text. For broadcast or streaming delivery, follow the supplied specification for format, channel/service and export. Keep a native caption track even when you also make animated graphics.</p>

<h2>Switching formats later</h2>
<p>Keep a copy of the sequence before changing formats. You can create captions again from the reviewed transcript with the required Format selected in Create captions. Review the result and its export options: styling and supported characters can differ between formats.</p>

<h2>Neither format animates</h2>
<p>Both formats support reusable Track Styles. Current Premiere versions also include a Single Word layout. Word-by-word highlighting, pop-ins and pill backgrounds need a graphics layer instead of a caption track, either built by hand or generated by a plugin.</p>
""")

